/* book Worked example: Q: An ideal Brayton cycle has a pressure ratio of 8. Air enters the compressor at 300 K and the turbine at 1200 K. Take cp = 1.005 kJ/kg·K and γ = 1.4. Find the compressor and turbine work, the net work, the heat added, the efficiency and the back work ratio. Then find the pressure ratio that gives the most net work and that work.
   1. x = rp(γ−1)/γ = 80.2857 = 1.811.
   2. T₂ = 300 × 1.811 = 543.4 K; T₄ = 1200/1.811 = 662.5 K.
   3. wc = 1.005 × (543.4 − 300) = 244.7 kJ/kg; wt = 1.005 × (1200 − 662.5) = 540.2 kJ/kg; wnet = 295.6 kJ/kg.
   4. qin = 1.005 × (1200 − 543.4) = 659.8 kJ/kg; η = 295.6/659.8 = 0.448. Check: 1 − 1/1.811 = 0.448.
   5. Back work ratio = 244.7/540.2 = 0.453: 45% of the turbine work drives the compressor.
   6. Best pressure ratio: rp,opt = (1200/300)1.75 = 41.75 = 11.31. Then T₂ = T₄ = √(300 × 1200) = 600 K and wmax = 1.005 × (34.64 − 17.32)² = 1.005 × 300 = 301.5 kJ/kg, at η = 1 − 300/600 = 50%.
   Answer: wc = 244.7 kJ/kg, wt = 540.2 kJ/kg, wnet = 295.6 kJ/kg, qin = 659.8 kJ/kg, η = 44.8%, back work ratio 0.453; rp,opt = 11.31 with wmax = 301.5 kJ/kg. */
