/* book Worked example 2: Q: A Stirling engine uses air between 300 K and 900 K with a volume ratio v₁/v₂ = 2; at state 1 (largest volume, 300 K) the pressure is 1 bar. Find the pressures, the net work and the efficiency (a) with a perfect regenerator, (b) with no regenerator, (c) with a regenerator that recovers 80% of the constant-volume heat. R = 0.287 and cv = 0.718 kJ/kg·K.
   1. Pressures: p₂ = 2 bar (isothermal, half the volume), p₃ = 2 × 900/300 = 6 bar, p₄ = 6/2 = 3 bar.
   2. Net work = R(TH − TL) ln 2 = 0.287 × 600 × 0.6931 = 119.4 kJ/kg.
   3. Heat in at TH: 0.287 × 900 × 0.6931 = 179.0 kJ/kg. Constant-volume heat: 0.718 × 600 = 430.8 kJ/kg.
   4. (a) η = 119.4/179.0 = 0.667, the Carnot value.
   5. (b) η = 119.4/(179.0 + 430.8) = 119.4/609.8 = 0.196.
   6. (c) Outside heat for 2→3 = 0.2 × 430.8 = 86.2 kJ/kg; η = 119.4/(179.0 + 86.2) = 0.450.
   Answer: p₂ = 2 bar, p₃ = 6 bar, p₄ = 3 bar; wnet = 119.4 kJ/kg; η = 66.7% with a perfect regenerator, 19.6% with none, 45.0% at 80% recovery. The regenerator is what makes the Stirling engine worth building. */
