/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Per kg of air, the swept volume is v₁ − v₂ (largest minus smallest specific volume).
   - pm = wnet/(v₁ − v₂) = Wnet/Vs
   - Here wnet is in kJ/kg and v in m³/kg, so pm comes out in kPa; Wnet is the net work per cycle in kJ and Vs the swept volume in m³.
   - pm,Otto = p₁ r (rγ−1 − 1)(rp − 1)/[(γ − 1)(r − 1)], with rp = p₃/p₂.
   - The MEP turns a cycle into engine power.
   - Every comparison starts from one fact: η = 1 − qout/qin, and in all three cycles the heat is rejected along the same kind of constant-volume line from the end of expansion back to state 1.
   - Same compression ratio and same heat input.
   - Same peak pressure and peak temperature.
   - The mean temperature idea gives the same answers in one line.
*/
